yglot community. From Spring and .NET, to Ruby and Node.js, there are active users of RabbitMQ out there writing in many different languages. It’s a polyglot world, and we’re connecting it all together!</p>
<h2 class="anchor anchorTargetStickyNavbar_Vzrq" id=project-updates>Project Updates<a href=#project-updates class=hash-link aria-label="Direct link to Project Updates" title="Direct link to Project Updates" translate=no>​</a></h2>
<ul>
<li class=""><a href=https://github.com/rabbitmq/rabbitmq-server/releases/tag/v3.7.11 target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class="">RabbitMQ 3.7.11</a> is available. It focuses on bug fixes and minor usability improvements. In particular, this release adds several new <a class="" href=/docs/man/rabbitmq-diagnostics.8>rabbitmq-diagnostics</a> commands useful for diagnostics and health checks. This is also the first release to require Erlang/OTP 20.3.</li>
<li class=""><a href=https://github.com/rabbitmq/rabbitmq-server/releases/tag/v3.8.0-beta.2 target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class="">Second beta</a> of RabbitMQ 3.8.0 is also out. <a class="" href=/docs/quorum-queues>Quorum queues</a> are now more stable, polished, and support more features. Give them a try!</li>
<li class=""><a href=https://hub.docker.com/_/rabbitmq target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class="">RabbitMQ Docker image</a> now provides the latest RabbitMQ and Erlang. The image is also <a href=https://github.com/docker-library/rabbitmq/pull/305 target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class="">automatically updated</a> when new RabbitMQ and Erlang releases come out.</li>
<li class="">The Docker image now can be used to easily <a href=https://github.com/docker-library/rabbitmq/pull/302 target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class="">test RabbitMQ 3.8.0 beta releases</a>.</li>
<li class=""><a href=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/rabbitmq-users/x3L2HPWuP1Y/5OH2iZmyFwAJ target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class="">Reactor RabbitMQ 1.1.0</a> GA has been released, with bug fixes, dependency upgrades, and new features.</li>
<li class=""><a href=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/rabbitmq-users/y20hnH1ZnMA/fpchrvR1FgAJ target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class="">Java client 5.6.0</a> for (Java 8+) and <a href=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/rabbitmq-users/Fl8MdG2ZfeU/wJoSROd1FgAJ target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class="">4.10.0</a> for (Java 6 & 7) have been released with bug fixes and new features.</li>
<li class="">Hop 3.1.0 GA has been released with a new feature and dependency upgrade. <a href=https://github.com/rabbitmq/hop target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class="">Hop</a> is a RabbitMQ HTTP API client for Java, Groovy, and other JVM languages.</li>
<li class=""><a href=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/rabbitmq-users/AvTbZgiQFa8/jIADaoNoFwAJ target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class="">JMS client 1.10.2</a> has been released with a bug fix.</li>
<li class=""><a class="" href=/docs/monitoring>Monitoring doc guide</a> has been expanded with a new section on health checks</li>
<li class="">CLI tools now provide a <a href=https://github.com/rabbitmq/rabbitmq-cli/issues/292 target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class="">range of progressive health checks</a> that operators can use individually or in combination</li>
<li class=""><a href=https://groups.google.com/forum/#!msg/rabbitmq-users/4azFra05kFI/Ha8jN1tEGAAJ target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" class="">OpenSUSE repositories were updated</a> to provide most recent RabbitMQ and Erlang versions</li>
</ul>
